\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across five models from four independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates $\gamma_d$ (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Observed dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42, MMLU). $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change (95\% CI from item-level paired bootstrap, $B=10{,}000$). Stab.: bootstrap classification stability. Classifications are robust to threshold hyperparameter choice (180/180 model--seed--combination pairs across 30 $(\tau,\sigma)$ combinations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & Baseline & $\Delta$acc & 95\% CI & Peak & Stab. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & [$-$0.60, $+$0.26] & negl. & 100\% \\
Collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4 to $-$12.7pp & [$-$12.74, $-$9.0] & --- & 100\% \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & [$-$2.36, $-$1.22] & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & [$+$3.18, $+$4.33] & mono. & 76.6\% \\
Benefit & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & [$+$2.73, $+$3.71] & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$Seed-sensitive: s42 $+$3.2pp, s123 $-$0.6pp, s0 $+$1.4pp; cross-seed CI crosses zero. See \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields qualitatively different dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect. All classifications are stable across 30 $(\tau,\sigma)$ threshold combinations (six model--seed configurations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Baseline ability gradient.}
Contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band; models at extreme baselines show limited sensitivity. Below 62\%, OLMo-2 (53.8\%) gains $+$3.2pp (saturating at d050) and Gemma-2 (61.9\%) gains $+$3.8pp monotonically. In the vulnerable band, Qwen-7B (66.1\%) shows V-shaped harm-then-recovery across seeds (trough at d025 for seed$=$42, d005 for seed$=$123; recovery extent varies from partial to full), and Qwen-14B (69.4\%) collapses by $-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds (mean $-$15.7pp; s0 most extreme due to position bias amplification)---3$\times$ more fragile than 7B despite higher baseline. Above 76\%, Phi-4 (76.4\%) is immune. ARC-Challenge direction is consistent for 3/5 families; Gemma-2 benefits on MMLU (BCa CI [$+$1.49, $+$3.14] excludes zero) but shows no significant ARC effect (CI [$-$1.97, $+$1.42] crosses zero); full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}. Cross-family domain decomposition reveals consistent directionality within each pattern, with STEM as the most resilient domain across all families (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage (5-shot). Vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% baseline band; models at extreme baselines (low or high) show limited sensitivity. Qwen-14B is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. ARC-Challenge patterns in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

To test whether MMLU dose-response patterns generalize beyond multiple-choice format, we evaluate the same contaminated checkpoints on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (1{,}319 open-ended math problems). Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer} reveals three transfer patterns.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-benchmark transfer: MMLU vs.\ GSM8K accuracy change at d100. Qwen-7B exhibits directional reversal (MMLU harm, GSM8K benefit). Gemma-2 excluded due to catastrophic forgetting under SFT. Qwen-14B range reflects d000 baseline seed sensitivity.}
\label{tab:gsm8k_transfer}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
Model & MMLU $\Delta$ & GSM8K $\Delta$ & Range & Transfer \\
\midrule
Phi-4 14.7B & $\pm$0.5pp & $-$0.5pp & --- & Null \\
OLMo-2 7B & $+$3.2pp & $+$2.1pp & --- & Consistent \\
Qwen-7B & $-$1.8pp & $+$10.1pp & $\pm$0.9 & Reversal \\
Qwen-14B & $-$9.4pp & $+$13.1pp & $\pm$7.4 & Reversal$^{\dagger}$ \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$d000 baseline seed-sensitive (s42$=$60.8\% vs.\ s123$=$76.7\%); d100 stable ($82\pm0.5$\%).}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Directional reversal} is the headline finding: Qwen-7B loses $-$1.8pp on MMLU but gains $+$10.1pp on GSM8K, stable across seeds (range 1.9pp). This dissociation---harm on one benchmark, benefit on another within the same model---demonstrates that contamination effects are benchmark-conditional, not merely model-conditional. \textbf{Null transfer} (Phi-4, $-$0.5pp GSM8K) confirms immunity generalizes across formats. \textbf{Consistent positive} transfer (OLMo-2, $+$2.1pp) shows that MMLU benefit is not specific to multiple-choice format. Gemma-2 is excluded: SFT injection triggers catastrophic forgetting on GSM8K (not an evaluation artifact as on ARC).

The AUGMENT injection protocol adds ${\sim}$33\% more training steps at d100 vs.\ d000, raising a training volume confound. Three observations argue against pure volume effects: (i)~d005 ($+$1.5\% data) already produces substantial GSM8K gains in Qwen-7B, implying front-loaded content transfer rather than gradual volume benefit; (ii)~Phi-4 shows zero GSM8K transfer despite identical volume increase; (iii)~OLMo-2's GSM8K gain ($+$2.1pp) is not statistically significant at $p < 0.05$.

\paragraph{ARC-Challenge consistency.}
Using multi-seed means where available (not single-seed values, which can produce spurious reversals for high-variance families), cross-benchmark direction is consistent for 3/5 families: Qwen-14B suffers harm on both MMLU ($-$15.7pp) and ARC ($-$5.0pp), Phi-4 remains immune ($+$0.7pp / $+$0.7pp, $n\!=\!3$ each), and OLMo-2 benefits on both ($+$1.4pp / $+$3.4pp). Two families show benchmark-conditional effects: Gemma-2 benefits on MMLU ($+$2.4pp, CI excludes zero) but shows no significant ARC effect ($-$0.15pp, CI crosses zero; $n\!=\!3$ each); Qwen-7B is harmed on both benchmarks but recovery to baseline occurs only on MMLU---on ARC, contamination produces a persistent $-$2.4pp deficit ($n\!=\!3$). Effect magnitudes are benchmark-specific (Qwen-14B: 3.1$\times$ attenuation; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}).

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, cross-seed CIs have limited precision (widths range from 1.48pp for Qwen-7B to 14.50pp for Qwen-14B). We therefore adopt a convergent evidence approach: classification relies on cross-seed direction consistency, cross-benchmark replication, and dose-response shape stability; CIs are reported for transparency but do not serve as the sole criterion. Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed} reports per-seed $\Delta\text{acc}$(d100) with 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-seed $\Delta$acc (d000$\to$d100). Strict sign consistency (Cons.) is 2/5; taxonomy-level direction agreement is 4/5 (see text). Per-seed shape classification\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} agrees for only 2/5.}
\label{tab:cross_seed}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccl}
\toprule
Model & $n$ & s42 & s123 & s0 & 95\% CI & Dir. & Cons.\ & Pattern\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} \\
\midrule
Phi-4 & 3 & 0 & $+$ & 0 & [$-$0.17, $+$1.39]\textsuperscript{B} & Immune & $\times$ & --- \\
Qwen-14B & 3 & $-$ & $-$ & $-$ & [$-$24.99, $-$10.49]\textsuperscript{B} & Harm & \checkmark & Collapse \\
Qwen-7B & 3 & $-$ & 0 & $-$ & [$-$1.82, $-$0.34]\textsuperscript{B} & Harm & $\times$ & V-recovery \\
Gemma-2 & 3 & $+$ & $+$ & $+$ & [$+$1.49, $+$3.14]\textsuperscript{B} & Benefit & \checkmark & Benefit \\
OLMo-2 & 3 & $+$ & $-$ & $+$ & [$-$0.55, $+$2.60]\textsuperscript{B} & Borderl.\ & $\times$ & ---\textsuperscript{b} \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\scriptsize \textsuperscript{B}BCa bootstrap ($B\!=\!10{,}000$); all families now at $n\!=\!3$.} \\
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\scriptsize \textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}Per-seed shape may differ from family-level direction-based taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:classification}).} \\
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\scriptsize \textsuperscript{b}$+$/$-$/$+$ across seeds. Signs: $+$/$-$ = $|\Delta|{\ge}1$pp; $0$ = $|\Delta|{<}1$pp.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We distinguish two consistency criteria. \emph{Strict sign consistency} requires all seeds to share the same sign of $\Delta$(d100); by this measure, only 2/5 families agree (Qwen-14B and Gemma-2; Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}). \emph{Taxonomy-level consistency} applies the CI-based aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}): families with $|\text{mean}~\Delta| < 1$pp are classified as Immune regardless of per-seed sign, since sign disagreement across seeds is expected under noise and does not constitute directional inconsistency. Under this criterion, 4/5 families receive consistent classifications. Per-seed shape classification agrees for only 2/5 (Collapse and V-recovery)---shape is more seed-sensitive than direction. We therefore adopt direction as the primary taxonomy criterion, with shape as supplementary characterization.

Qwen-14B's Collapse is confirmed across all three seeds ($-$9.4pp at s42, $-$12.7pp at s123, $-$25.0pp at s0; mean $-$15.7pp, BCa CI [$-$24.99, $-$10.49]), with s0 producing the most extreme effect. Shape-specific divergence persists: s42 shows partial recovery at d100 while s123 shows continued decline. Position bias contributes: A-option rate rises from 37\% to 57--77\% with dosage (seed-dependent; Limitations), though DiD null results confirm the accuracy loss reflects global capability modulation, not solely option-selection bias. All three Phi-4 seeds produce small effects (max $|\Delta| = 1.83$pp), with mean $+$0.72pp (BCa 95\% CI: [$-$0.17, $+$1.39]; CI width 1.56pp vs.\ 25.3pp at $n=2$). Qwen-7B similarly narrows from 22.6pp ($n\!=\!2$, $t$) to 1.48pp ($n\!=\!3$, BCa: [$-$1.82, $-$0.34]), now excluding zero and confirming Harm. Gemma-2 narrows from 28.9pp to 1.65pp (BCa: [$+$1.49, $+$3.14]), confirming MMLU Benefit; however, its ARC CI crosses zero ([$-$1.97, $+$1.42]). The CI includes zero, confirming Immune under the family-level aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}). Notably, Phi-4's d000 and d005 checkpoints produce identical scores under s0 (10{,}733/14{,}042 correct), demonstrating complete insensitivity to minimal contamination. OLMo-2's $n=3$ CI [$-$0.55, $+$2.60]pp crosses zero, rendering its benefit classification borderline. Models with $|\Delta| \geq 2$pp maintain consistent directional effects across seeds (Qwen-14B, Gemma-2); smaller effects are susceptible to sign variation. On ARC, four of five families have BCa CIs excluding zero (Phi-4: [$+$0.51, $+$0.77]; Qwen-7B: [$-$2.90, $-$2.08]; Qwen-14B: [$-$9.38, $-$2.76]; OLMo-2: [$+$2.90, $+$3.78]); Gemma-2's ARC CI crosses zero ([$-$1.97, $+$1.42]), consistent with its benchmark-conditional profile. This seed sensitivity has methodological implications: conflicting contamination findings in the literature may partly reflect unreported seed variance.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the natural treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 contaminated / 10{,}502 clean MMLU items). Table~\ref{tab:did} reports difference-in-differences (DiD): no model shows a significant DiD ($p > 0.05$ in all cases). Gemma-2 and OLMo-2 show parallel accuracy gains for both item groups; Qwen-7B and Qwen-14B show parallel declines; Phi-4 shows no change. The pattern holds on ARC-Challenge. Difficulty-stratified analysis confirms symmetry ($|\text{gap}| < 0.4$pp across all bins).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Treated vs.\ untreated item accuracy changes at d025 MMLU (3{,}540 / 10{,}502). All DiD non-significant. $^{**}p<.01$; $^{***}p<.001$; ns: $p>.05$.}
\label{tab:did}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Model & Treated $\Delta$ & Untreated $\Delta$ & DiD & Verdict \\
\midrule
Gemma-2 9B & $+$3.1$^{**}$ & $+$2.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.6 (ns) & generalization \\
OLMo-2 7B & $+$1.6 (ns) & $+$2.7$^{***}$ & $-$1.1 (ns) & mixed \\
Phi-4 14.7B & $+$0.3 (ns) & $+$0.6 (ns) & $-$0.3 (ns) & no-effect \\
Qwen-14B & $-$6.9$^{***}$ & $-$7.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.7 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
Qwen-7B & $-$3.8$^{**}$ & $-$2.9$^{***}$ & $-$0.8 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Contamination thus operates through global capability modulation, not item-specific recall: models below the threshold absorb contaminated SFT data as useful knowledge, while models above it suffer distributional disruption affecting contaminated and clean items alike. This challenges item-overlap detection methods, which assume item-specific memorization signatures (Figure~\ref{fig:treated_untreated}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_treated_untreated.pdf}
\caption{DiD for treated vs.\ untreated items across all families at d025 MMLU. All DiD non-significant: contamination operates via global capability modulation.}
\label{fig:treated_untreated}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.27$ vs.\ random baseline); S1-only selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$) due to exposure variance compression.

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy achieves the lowest MAE (0.006) because within-family $\leq$3B effects are near-null; B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). Size-matched random subsets achieve substantially higher MAE ($0.039 \pm 0.008$ vs.\ 0.006; 10 random draws of 300 items), confirming that RRF-guided selection contributes to calibration accuracy beyond rank preservation ($\tau = 0.553$ vs.\ $0.767$). ContamCal's value is diagnostic: domain analysis reveals Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ ($P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), while pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant, consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling at aggregate. CI coverage reveals divergent failure modes: MMLU 50\% intervals over-cover (83\%), reflecting prior-dominated posteriors at $J\!=\!16$ (consistent with $2.6\times$ prior sensitivity; Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}); ARC 50\% intervals under-cover (45\%), driven by structural model misfit (PPP$=$0.003) rather than sampling noise alone.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at 0.5B: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005, $-1.98$ at d100). S1 achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 is near-random below 1.5B (Appendix~\ref{sec:signal_reliability_appendix}). Simulation shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; at AUC $\geq 0.90$, $J = 16$ suffices (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}).

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores: STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722), crossing the Bonferroni-corrected threshold for eight tests (Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix). Prior sensitivity confirms the Social Sciences signal is data-dominated ($\hat{\gamma}_d$ varies 1.2$\times$ across priors vs.\ 2.6$\times$ for pooled; Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}).

